  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 60af0432a3c869e48a591344a8dafd71, type: 3}
  m_Name: PatPre_6
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  question: "You\u2019ve been picking your oncologist\u2019s brain about how new
    cancer treatments are developed, and the topic of animal studies comes up. You\u2019ve
    always been curious why researchers test new treatments in animals before people
    and how this step might affect the safety and effectiveness of the treatments
    that could eventually be available to you or other patients.\r\n\r\nWhy is it
    important for researchers to test new treatments in animals (in vivo) during
    preclinical research?\r\n"
  explanation: Animal studies are a key step in protecting patients. They help researchers
    see how a treatment behaves in a living body, including potential side effects,
    safe dosage, and how organs or systems may respond. Understanding these effects
    in animals first allows scientists to design safer and more focused clinical
    trials, increasing the likelihood that new treatments will be both effective
    and safe for the people who ultimately need them.
  correctAnswer: They help researchers understand how a treatment might affect organs,
    systems, and overall health in a living body before human testing
  incorrectAnswer1: They show researchers if a new treatment will work in patients
  incorrectAnswer2: They allow researchers to bypass lab testing and move forward
    into clinical trials faster
  incorrectAnswer3: They show researchers how diverse patient populations will respond
    to a new treatment
  questionRole: Patient
  questionType: MultipleChoice
  questionStage: Preclinical
